    body: [
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "Hello, Rumana! What are these small plants in your basket?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "Hello, Nabila. They are saplings of mango, guava and neem. I bought them from the tree fair near our school." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "What will you do with so many?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "I shall plant them around our house. It is the rainy season, the best time for planting trees." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "Why are you so interested in planting trees?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "Because trees are our best friends. We cannot live without them." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "How do you say that?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "Trees give us oxygen, without which we cannot breathe even for a few minutes. They absorb the carbon dioxide that we breathe out, and so they keep the air pure." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "I knew that. What else do they do for us?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "They give us fruit, food, timber, fuel and medicine. Our houses, furniture and boats are made of wood. Cattle eat their leaves as fodder, and many trees like neem and arjun have medicinal value." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "Do they help the weather as well?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "Certainly. Trees bring rain and control the temperature. Their roots hold the soil and prevent erosion. The Sundarbans protect our coastal people from cyclones like Sidr and Aila." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "Then what will happen if we go on cutting down trees?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "The country will turn into a desert. The temperature will rise, the rainfall will decrease, and floods and droughts will become more frequent. The ecological balance will be destroyed." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "How much forest should a country have?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "Experts say that a country should have at least twenty-five per cent of its land under forest. We have much less than that." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "That is a serious matter. What should we do?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "We must plant more trees and stop cutting them down carelessly. There is a lot of empty land beside roads, ponds, schools and offices. Even in cities, trees can be planted on rooftops." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "And the government?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "The government arranges tree fairs and social forestation programmes every year. But everyone must come forward. If one tree is cut, at least two should be planted in its place." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "You have inspired me. Will you give me two of your saplings?" },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "With great pleasure. Take this mango and this neem. Plant them today and water them regularly." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Nabila", text: "I shall. Thank you very much, Rumana." },
      { type: "dialogue", speaker: "Rumana", text: "You are welcome. Goodbye." },
    ],
